            <div class="bg-neutral-50 rounded-lg p-6 border border-neutral-200">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-brand-dark mb-3">Nail-Down Installation</h3>
              <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed text-sm">
                Commonly used for solid hardwood, this method secures boards directly to a plywood
                subfloor using nails or staples. It creates a strong, traditional installation with
                a solid feel underfoot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-neutral-50 rounded-lg p-6 border border-neutral-200">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-brand-dark mb-3">Glue-Down Installation</h3>
              <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed text-sm">
                Ideal for engineered wood and concrete subfloors, glue-down installation provides
                excellent stability and reduces movement. This method is often used in commercial
                spaces or areas with high foot traffic.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-neutral-50 rounded-lg p-6 border border-neutral-200">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-brand-dark mb-3">Floating Installation</h3>
              <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed text-sm">
                Floating floors are not attached to the subfloor but instead lock together over an
                underlayment. This method is quick, versatile, and works well for engineered wood
                over concrete or radiant heat systems.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
